<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colonial Office</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Colonial Office was the British government <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> that administered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s colonies, separated from the War Office in 1854. Crown colonies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> were governed by officials who reported to the Secretary of State for the Colonies. It took over territories such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/straits-settlements">Straits Settlements</a> and, after company rule ended, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia</a>, and was merged into the Commonwealth Office in 1966.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colonial-office.jpg" alt="Colonial Office" width="320" height="185" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Illustrated London News · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foreign_and_India_Offices,_London,_1866_ILN.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before 1768 colonial affairs belonged to the Secretary of State for the Southern Department and the Board of Trade and Plantations, a committee of the Privy Council. A separate American or Colonial Department was set up in 1768 to deal with British America, but after the loss of the Thirteen Colonies it was abolished in 1782 and its work passed to the Home Office. In 1801, in the lead-up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, colonial business moved to the War Office, renamed the War and Colonial Office, under a Secretary of State for War and the Colonies.</p><p>An Under-Secretary of State for the Colonies was created in 1825, first held by Robert William Hay. From 1824 the empire outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, the Australian colonies and the eastern colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1854 the War and Colonial Office was split into the War Office and a new Colonial Office under the Secretary of State for the Colonies. It never ran all British possessions: from 1858 the India Office governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a> and nearby territories, and informal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorates</a> such as the Khedivate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> were left to the Foreign Office. The growing independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominions</a> after the 1907 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-conference">Imperial Conference</a> led to a Dominion Division inside the office, and from 1925 there was a separate Secretary of State for Dominion Affairs.</p><p>After the Cairo Conference of March 1921 the Colonial Office took over the administration of the Palestine Mandate from the Foreign Office. On 16 April 1947 the Irgun planted a bomb at the office that failed to explode. In 1966 the Colonial Office and the Commonwealth Relations Office were merged as the Commonwealth Office, which two years later joined the Foreign Office as the Foreign and Commonwealth Office.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From 1862 the office published historical and statistical information on the colonies in The Colonial Office List, called The Dominions Office and Colonial Office List between 1926 and 1940 and later the Commonwealth Relations Office Year Book and Commonwealth Office Year Book. An edited version was also printed by Waterlow and Sons, and the two can be hard to tell apart in library catalogues. The office worked from what is now the Foreign and Commonwealth Office Main Building in Whitehall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colonial Office” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1911 the company joined its two territories into Northern Rhodesia, which passed to the British <b>Colonial Office</b> in 1924.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sixth Frontier War of 1834–1836 led Governor Benjamin D’Urban to annex the Province of Queen Adelaide, an annexation reversed by the <b>Colonial Office</b> in 1836.</blockquote><a href="/empire/cape-colony">Cape Colony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Colonial Office</b>'s statement of 1922 said Palestine as a whole was not to become a Jewish national home, a reading the Zionist Executive then accepted.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mandatory-palestine">Mandatory Palestine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>At the top stood a metropolitan authority: Spain’s Council of the Indies from 1524, Britain’s <b>Colonial Office</b>, France’s ministry of the colonies, or a chartered company such as the English East India Company (1600) or the Dutch East India Company (1602), which could make war and treaties in…</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>In a 1922 referendum they rejected union with South Africa, and in 1923 Southern Rhodesia became a self-governing British colony; Northern Rhodesia passed to the <b>Colonial Office</b> in 1924, though the company kept mineral rights.</blockquote><a href="/state/company-rule-in-rhodesia">Company rule in Rhodesia</a></li><li><blockquote>It was governed by the Foreign Office rather than the <b>Colonial Office</b>, and its officials began pushing into the hinterland of the Niger Delta.</blockquote><a href="/state/niger-coast-protectorate">Niger Coast Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
